    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="choose">Choosing where backups go</h2>
  <p>Projects back up with <strong>Git Backup</strong>. The status bar shows where, and when the last backup was, for example <em>GitHub · 10m</em>. Click it to set up backups, or click <strong>Back up now</strong> (the circular arrows) to take one immediately. If a backup needs your attention, the status bar says so.</p>
  <div class="note">
    <p>Making backups needs an active trial or license. Restoring one you already made always works.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="git">Git Backup</h2>
  <p>Git Backup keeps a history of your project in a hidden folder next to the project file, on your own computer. You don't need to know anything about Git, but it does need to be installed: get it free from <a href="https://git-scm.com/downloads">git-scm.com</a>. On a Mac, it's included with Apple's Command Line Tools.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <span class="menu">Project → Git Backup…</span>.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Turn On Backup</strong>. Ravenburst takes the first backup straight away.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Back Up Now</strong> whenever you like, or tick <strong>Back up automatically</strong> and choose how often: every 15 minutes, 30 minutes, hour or 2 hours (30 minutes by default). Automatic backup also runs once more when you quit.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Each backup appears in the panel's list with its date. A backup is only made when something has changed.</p>
  <p><strong>Advanced</strong> holds the Git controls, for people who want them: a remote address, Push, Pull, and a backup with your own message.</p>
  <div class="note warn">
    <p>Git Backup works on the folder that holds your project file. Several projects in one folder can all be backed up, each with its own list of backups, but they share one online copy, and the panel says so. To keep projects completely separate, give each its own folder.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="online">Keeping a copy online</h2>
  <p>A backup on the same computer won't survive the computer. To keep a copy online as well:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Create a free account with GitHub, GitLab, Bitbucket, Codeberg or SourceHut.</li>
    <li>Create a new, <strong>empty</strong> private repository (don't add a README).</li>
    <li>In the Git Backup panel, under <strong>Keep a copy online</strong>, paste the repository's address, click <strong>Test</strong>, then <strong>Use This</strong>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>From then on, every backup is also sent there. Sign-in uses your computer's own Git credentials; if Ravenburst can't sign in, install <a href="https://github.com/git-ecosystem/git-credential-manager">Git Credential Manager</a> and try again.</p>
  <p>If the online copy and your computer have both changed (for example, you wrote on two computers), Ravenburst stops and asks rather than merging. Restore the version you want to keep from the list of backups.</p>

  <h2 id="restore">Restoring</h2>
  <p>In either panel, click <strong>Restore</strong> next to a backup and choose a name and place for it. The backup is saved as a <strong>new project file</strong> (for example <code>My Novel-restored.ravenburst</code>); the project you have open is never overwritten. Open the restored file with <span class="menu">File → Open Project…</span> and copy back what you need.</p>
  <p>For one document rather than the whole project, <a href="/docs/snapshots">snapshots</a> are quicker.</p>

  <h2 id="manual">Copying the file yourself</h2>
  <p>Everything in a project is in its <code>.ravenburst</code> file, so copying that file somewhere safe (a USB drive, another computer, a folder that your cloud service syncs) is a complete backup. Close Ravenburst first so the copy is up to date.</p>
